<!-- OpenUnlock — Tasker project (v0.2.2).
     THREE ways Tasker drives Shizuku here, all of them real on this phone:
     A. Deep links — Tasker opens openunlock:// URIs and the APP does the
        Shizuku work (same action as the on-screen button). Map:
          openunlock://frp           -> FRP status (Shizuku: getprop/settings/ls)
          openunlock://imei          -> IMEI read (Shizuku: dumpsys / service call)
          openunlock://firmware      -> firmware ZIP picker (Shizuku getprop if allowed)
          openunlock://profiles      -> all profiles (offline, no Shizuku)
          openunlock://info          -> device info + profile (Shizuku: getprop)
          openunlock://wireless-adb  -> run the wireless-ADB flow (Shizuku + adb)
          openunlock://adb-devices   -> print `adb devices` (Shizuku + adb)
          openunlock://shizuku-status -> print the Shizuku state (no Shizuku needed)
          openunlock://pair?code=123456 -> pair + connect (Shizuku + adb)
          openunlock://nfc-reader    -> arm the NFC reader (no Shizuku needed)
     B. Tasker Run Shell with "Use Shizuku" ticked — Tasker itself runs
        shell commands as the ADB shell user (uid 2000). This is the
        phone's established pattern (Battery Saver / Grant Permissions /
        Tunnel Status Check in the main project). The XML encoding of the
        "Use Shizuku" tick is NOT published anywhere checkable, so it is
        NOT set in this file: after importing, open each Run Shell action
        and tick "Use Shizuku" (or turn on Tasker Preferences > Misc >
        "Use Shizuku By Default"). Without the tick the command runs as
        plain Tasker shell and Shizuku checks read FAIL — the OU Check
        Shizuku task says so in its own notification.
     C. rish via Termux RUN_COMMAND — for commands that need the Termux
        environment (the adb binary, the Python CLI). rish is the Shizuku
        shell inside Termux: export it from the Shizuku app ("Use Shizuku
        in terminal apps", edit PKG to com.termux) and put it where Termux
        finds it ($PREFIX/bin/rish or ~/rish). The rish tasks write their
        script to /storage/emulated/0/Tasker/ou-rish-*.sh, fire it with
        the documented com.termux.RUN_COMMAND service intent, and the
        script posts its result with termux-notification AND saves it to
        /storage/emulated/0/Tasker/ou-rish-*.txt — RUN_COMMAND runs in
        the background and cannot return output to Tasker, so the file
        + notification is the result channel. OU Setup Check reports
        exactly what is missing (rish / adb / python / ~/openunlock).
     Primary trigger is still the OpenUnlock app itself in NFC reader
     mode: tap a client phone to the host phone and the app detects it
     and runs its default action + the wireless-ADB flow. The NFC
     profiles below are the FALLBACK: each fires on ANY NFC tag (event
     code 600, blank tag ID) and opens the matching deep link. A client
     phone randomizes its UID per tap, so an NFC profile can never
     reliably match one specific client — and all NFC profiles fire at
     once on any tag, so enable only the ones you want and disable the
     rest. The profile-less tasks (OU Wireless ADB - start/status,
     OU Pair client, OU Check Shizuku, the OU rish tasks, OU Setup
     Check, OU Check All) are run by hand from Tasker's Tasks tab, or
     from the existing dispatcher by name.
     Every task logs one line to /storage/emulated/0/Tasker/events.csv,
     so a run can be proven from the log.
     Action codes used here are ONLY the ones in the real Tasker 6.2.26
     exports on this machine: Run Shell 123, Notify 523, Perform Task
     130, NFC Tag event 600. No If/Else, Wait, or Read File actions —
     conditionals are done inside the shell commands themselves.
     Import: Tasker → Menu → Data → Import Project → this file.
     After import, re-import is needed after any XML change. -->
